Renux in development - Release Notes

Renux is a general-purpose operating system, easy to maintain and
portable, built with Zig alone: install Zig, build the whole system.

This is the first snapshot of Renux.  It is experimental: do not
install it on a machine that holds data you need.


What is in it

  - The kernel, the EFI loader and the whole userland are built by
    `zig build` from the Renux source tree, with Zig 0.17 as the
    compiler.
  - Renux is based on FreeBSD 15.1 and follows FreeBSD's development.
  - Zig is the system's compiler: zig, cc and c++ compile Zig, C and
    C++ from the first boot.
  - The installer speaks English, Spanish and Portuguese, and has
    these release notes.  It sets up the network and can install the
    package manager (pkg) from the Internet, so that "pkg install"
    works from the first boot.
  - The console:
      - the text cursor is an underline;
      - full-screen programs (vi, less, htop) get a screen of their
        own and give the previous one back when they quit;
      - clear also empties the history.
  - The login welcomes to Renux.


Known limitations

  - amd64 and UEFI only: Renux does not boot by BIOS (CSM, Legacy).
  - UFS only: the kernel has no modules yet, so there is no ZFS, and
    the drivers FreeBSD ships as modules (some Wi-Fi cards, for
    instance) are missing.
  - In Spanish and Portuguese, the names of keymaps and of most time
    zones are still in English.
  - The system's compiler, Zig 0.17 (with cc, c++, ld, ar and ranlib,
    which run it), is ziglang.org's build for FreeBSD; it is not yet
    built from the Renux tree.


